\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models built on large vision-language models (VLMs) inherit strong semantic grounding, but VLM pre-training emphasizes high-level understanding and neglects low-level spatial cues and physical dynamics that precise manipulation needs. Prediction-based policies show the opposite profile: future-frame pre-training improves spatial grounding but lacks semantic robustness. We present UP-VLA, a single autoregressive VLA that trains multi-modal understanding (MMU) and future image prediction jointly. A unified prompting and attention-masking scheme lets continuous MMU tokens and discrete prediction tokens share one language-model backbone, and a joint prediction-and-understanding mechanism conditions action generation on a generated scene description and on predicted future image tokens. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA reaches an average completed length of 4.08, a 33\% improvement over GR-1 (3.06), and on ABCD$\rightarrow$D it reaches 4.42 against 4.21 for GR-1. On a Franka Panda, it improves both unseen-object grasping and precision tasks over the ablated variants. Ablations show that prediction, Bridge pre-training and MMU each contribute.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Language-conditioned robot policies have moved from hand-built instruction parsing \citep{macmahon2006walk,tellex2011understanding} and learned language-conditioned imitation \citep{stepputtis2020language,jang2022bc,shridhar2022cliport} toward large pre-trained models. Vision-language-action (VLA) models initialize a policy from a VLM so that web-scale semantic knowledge transfers to control: RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt} and OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla} fine-tune a VLM to emit actions, and PaLM-E \citep{driess2023palm} injects embodied observations into a language model. These models generalize well to new objects and instructions.

Their pre-training objective is the limitation. VLMs are trained mostly on tasks such as visual question answering, which reward high-level semantics and give little incentive to encode depth, size, relative pose, or how a scene will change under an action \citep{chen2024spatialvlm,balazadeh2024synthetic}. Such cues are what precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes depend on. A second family, prediction-based policies, pre-trains on future frames or videos \citep{wu2023unleashing,black2023zero,du2024learning}; it captures spatial and physical structure, yet it learns from robot video without broad language-image data and is less robust semantically. Figure~\ref{fig:motivation} summarizes this dichotomy and our approach.

