We propose \textbf{UP-VLA}, a unified VLA that keeps the strengths of both families in one autoregressive backbone. It is trained with (1) MMU on large-scale image-text data and (2) future visual prediction on robot video data, and at action time it conditions on both a language scene description and predicted future image tokens. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item UP-VLA, a VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding and future prediction in one autoregressive framework.
\item A unified prompting and attention-masking strategy that mixes continuous MMU image tokens and discrete predictive image tokens in a single LLM.
\item A joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism that conditions actions on both a generated scene description and predicted futures.
\item State-of-the-art results on CALVIN (average length 4.08 on ABC$\rightarrow$D, +33\% over GR-1), real-robot gains in semantic generalization and precision tasks, and ablations isolating each component.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Works}
\paragraph{VLM-based VLAs.} Many systems adapt pre-trained language or vision-language models to robotics. Earlier work uses language models as planners or grounders \citep{ahn2022can,huang2022inner,liang2023code,huang2023voxposer}; later work trains the model to output actions directly, as in RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt}, OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla}, RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision}, 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d}, TraceVLA \citep{zheng2024tracevla}, and the flow-based $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi_0}. Extensions target hierarchy \citep{belkhale2024rt,zhang2024hirt}, speech instructions \citep{zhao2025vlas}, humanoids and quadrupeds \citep{ding2025humanoid,ding2024quar}, and dual-system designs \citep{cui2025openhelix}. These inherit VLM semantics but not VLM-scale spatial or dynamics supervision; UP-VLA adds an explicit future-prediction objective to the same kind of backbone.

\paragraph{Prediction-based policies.} Another line uses generative models of future observations. SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} edits images with a pre-trained diffusion model to produce subgoals, UniPi \citep{du2024learning} generates text-conditioned videos and recovers actions, GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} pre-trains a GPT-style model on large-scale video before robot fine-tuning, and joint denoising of video and actions has also been studied \citep{guo2024prediction,zhang2025gevrm}. IGOR \citep{chen2024igorimagegoalrepresentationsatomic} learns image-goal representations from human and robot data. These capture visual dynamics but do not use language-image understanding data; UP-VLA retains MMU training alongside prediction.

\paragraph{Unified understanding and generation.} Several multimodal models handle comprehension and image generation in one network \citep{xie2024show,dong2023dreamllm,ge2024seed}, building on discrete image tokenizers \citep{esser2021taming} and autoregressive image generation \citep{sun2024autoregressive}. Show-o in particular combines autoregressive text and discrete image tokens with different attention patterns. We adapt this idea to embodied control, adding future prediction from robot data and an action output.

\paragraph{Spatial and physical limits of VLMs.} Recent studies show that VLMs struggle with spatial relations and physical dynamics \citep{chen2024spatialvlm,wen2024can,ghaffari2024exploring,li2024multimodal}. This motivates our choice to supplement understanding with predictive pre-training rather than relying on understanding alone.

\section{Preliminaries}
We consider language-conditioned manipulation: given an instruction $L$ and an observation $O_t$, the policy outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. We use two image pathways. A continuous pathway encodes an image with CLIP-ViT \citep{radford2021learning} and an MLP projection $E_1$ into the language embedding space, giving image tokens $u$. A discrete pathway uses a VQ-GAN encoder $E_2$ \citep{esser2021taming} to map an image to codebook indices $v$; a VQ decoder reconstructs images from predicted indices. The backbone is the Phi-1.5 language model \citep{li2023textbooks}. All encoders are frozen and the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Overview}
Figure~\ref{fig:compare} contrasts UP-VLA with VLM-based VLAs and prediction-based models, and Figure~\ref{fig:overview} gives the architecture. One Phi-1.5 backbone performs multi-modal understanding, future image token prediction and action generation. Training has two stages: pre-training on MMU data (LLaVA-tuning-665k, \citealp{liu2024visual}) and prediction data (25k Bridge demonstrations, \citealp{walke2023bridgedata}), then action fine-tuning on downstream robot data with joint prediction-and-understanding prompts.

