\subsection{Unified prompting and attention masks}
The two objectives need different input layouts, shown in Figure~\ref{fig:prompt}.

\textbf{MMU.} The input is $\{[\text{MMU}], u, l\}$. Image tokens precede text so that they attend to each other, and the model predicts the next language tokens: $\hat H_L=\pi_\theta^{\text{MMU}}(I,L)$.

\textbf{Future prediction (PRE).} The input is $\{[\text{PRE}], l, v_t\}$. Image tokens come after the text so that they attend to all earlier tokens, and future image tokens are predicted at aligned positions rather than as a next-token continuation: $\hat H_{O_{t+\Delta t}}=\pi_\theta^{\text{PRE}}(O_t,L)$.

\textbf{Action learning.} For robot data, the sequence concatenates the MMU-derived scene description and visual tokens with the instruction, so action tokens attend to both semantic (MMU) tokens and visual predictive tokens.

