\subsection{Joint prediction and understanding for action}
Given $(O_t,L)$, UP-VLA first generates a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\text{MMU}}(O_t,L_{\text{prompt}})$ with a prompt such as ``describe this image'', embeds additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$, and forms the extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$. It then jointly predicts future image tokens and actions, $(\hat H_{O_{t+\Delta t}},\hat H_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}})=\pi_\theta^{\text{PRE}}(O_t,L')$. A lightweight policy head (a single-layer attention pooling, MAP, followed by an MLP) maps the action tokens to continuous actions: $\hat a_{t:t+\Delta t}=\text{MLP}(\text{MAP}(\hat H_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}}))$. The description supplies semantics; the predicted future supplies spatial and dynamics information.

\subsection{Training objectives}
The MMU loss is the log-likelihood of text tokens given image tokens,
$\mathcal L_{\text{MMU}}=\sum_i \log p_\theta(l_i\mid u,l_{1:i-1})$.
The prediction loss models $P(O_{t+\Delta t}\mid O_t,L)=\prod_{j=1}^{M}p_\theta(v'_j\mid l,v_{1:M})$ over the $M$ future image tokens, $\mathcal L_{\text{PRE}}=\sum_j\log p_\theta(v'_j\mid l,v_{1:M})$. The action loss combines a squared error on the position component with binary cross-entropy on the gripper-end component, $\mathcal L_{\text{ACT}}=\lVert\hat a_{\text{pos}}-a_{\text{pos}}\rVert_2^2+\text{BCE}(\hat a_{\text{end}},a_{\text{end}})$. The total objective is $\mathcal L=\lambda_1\mathcal L_{\text{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal L_{\text{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal L_{\text{ACT}}$ (the likelihood terms are maximized, i.e.\ used as cross-entropy losses). In stage~1, MMU and PRE data are mixed with their task-specific prompts and masks; in stage~2, UP-VLA is fine-tuned on robot data with joint prompts while MMU co-training continues to retain semantic ability.

\subsection{Test-time procedure}
At each step the model receives $O_t$ and $L$, generates $s$ by MMU, forms $L'$, runs the prediction pathway to get future tokens and action tokens, decodes actions with MAP+MLP, executes them, and repeats autoregressively.

\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Setup}
\textbf{Simulation.} We use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} in the ABC$\rightarrow$D and ABCD$\rightarrow$D splits (scenes A--D) and report the success rate of completing $1$--$5$ chained tasks and the average completed length. \textbf{Real world.} A Franka Emika Panda with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills; we test seen tasks, unseen-object grasping and precise operations (e.g.\ cable routing, small-object grasping), with 20 trials per task (Figure~\ref{fig:env}). \textbf{Baselines.} VLA-based: RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision}, 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d}, and UP-VLA-RT-2, our RT-2-style \citep{brohan2023rt} variant on the same backbone; RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt}. Prediction-based: GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero}, Uni-Pi \citep{du2024learning}, 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d}, and Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy}. We also train UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu, a GR-1-like model with the same Phi-1.5 backbone but no MMU. Other hyperparameters (e.g.\ $\lambda$ values, optimizer, schedule) are not reported in our source material.

