Table~\ref{table:ablate} removes one component at a time. Removing prediction is most damaging in simulation (4.08 to 1.44), and removing Bridge pre-training also costs much (2.74), so predictive pre-training on 25k demonstrations matters for spatial and dynamics grounding. Removing MMU barely changes simulation (3.89) or seen real tasks (0.85) but cuts unseen-object success from 0.58 to 0.20, pointing to MMU as the source of semantic generalization. Dropping only the MMU-derived scene-description condition lowers unseen success to 0.50 and simulation to 3.99, so conditioning actions on the description helps beyond co-training alone. Note that the ablation row ``w/o MMU'' in Table~\ref{table:ablate} and the UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu bars in Figure~\ref{fig:real} are reported as given and are not identical numbers; the source material does not explain the difference.

