\section{Limitations}
Predicted images show background color artifacts in unseen scenes, indicating insufficient visual-generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. MMU sometimes misclassifies objects, reflecting the limits of the small backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and curated data could reduce this. Joint tasks lengthen sequences and training, so parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory may be needed. Finally, UP-VLA uses single-camera RGB; 3D inputs, multiple views or explicit physics modeling are untested. Real-world results use 20 trials per task, so differences of a few points are within sampling noise, and some baseline numbers are copied from other papers' protocols.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future image prediction in one autoregressive VLA and conditions action on both a scene description and predicted futures. It reaches 4.08 average length on CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D, and improves real-world unseen-object and precision tasks. Ablations attribute spatial and long-horizon gains to prediction and semantic generalization to MMU. The evidence is limited to CALVIN and one real-robot setup with modest trial counts, and it does not show that the approach scales to larger backbones.

